The '''Yimas language''' is spoken by the Yimas people, who populate the [[Sepik River]] Basin region of [[Papua New Guinea]].<ref name=e25/> It is spoken primarily in Yimas village ({{coord|-4.680562|143.548847|type:city_region:PG|name=Yimas 1}}), [[Karawari Rural LLG]], [[East Sepik Province]].<ref name="UN-PNG">{{cite web |url=https://data.humdata.org/dataset/village-coordinates-lookup |title=Papua New Guinea Village Coordinates Lookup |author=United Nations in Papua New Guinea |work=Humanitarian Data Exchange |version=1.31.9 |date=2018}}</ref> It is a member of the [[Ramu - Lower Sepik|Lower-Sepik language family]].<ref name=Foley>{{cite book |last1=Foley |first1=William A. |title=The Yimas Language of New Guinea |date=1991 |publisher=Stanford University Press |location=Stanford, CA |isbn=9780804715829}}</ref>{{Rp|1}} All 250-300 speakers of Yimas live in two villages along the lower reaches of the Arafundi River, which stems from a tributary of the Sepik River known as the Karawari River.<ref name=e25/><ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|7}}

Yimas is a [[polysynthetic language]] with (somewhat) free [[word order]], and is an [[ergative-absolutive]] language [[morphology (linguistics)|morphologically]] but not [[syntax|syntactically]], although it has several other case-like relations encoded on its verbs. It has ten main noun classes (genders), and a unique number system. Four of the noun classes are semantically determined (male humans, female humans, higher animals, plants and plant material) whereas the rest are assigned on phonological bases.<ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|119}}

It is an [[endangered language]], being widely replaced by [[Tok Pisin]], and to a lesser extent, English. It is unclear if any children are native Yimas speakers. However, a '''Yimas pidgin''' was once used as a contact language with speakers of [[Alamblak language|Alamblak]] and [[Arafundi language|Arafundi]].{{citation needed|date=August 2019}} Although it is still used in face-to-face conversation, it is considered a threatened language on the ''[[Ethnologue]]'' endangerment scale, with a rating of 6b.<ref name=e25/><ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|4–5}}

== Phonology ==

Yimas has a total of 18 [[phonemes]]. Below are the vowel and consonant inventories, which are represented using [[International Phonetic Alphabet]] (IPA) symbols.

=== Consonants ===

