The phonemic status of the palatal consonants /c/, /ɲ/ and /ʎ/ is not entirely clear. In general their appearance is predictable; they arise primarily through [[Palatalization (phonetics)|palatalization]] of the alveolar consonants /t/, /n/, and /r/. However, there are a few words in which these consonants must be regarded as underlyingly palatal. Examples include ''akulɨm'' 'wrist', ''ɨɲcɨt'' 'urine', and other words, though these historically go back to alveolar consonants, as can be seen in their cognates in [[Karawari language|Karawari]] (''awku'''ri'''m'' 'wrist' and ''sɨ'''ndi''''' 'urine').<ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|38}}

Adjacent nasals and plosives are usually [[homorganic]]. Other combinations such as ''mt'', ''mk'', ''np'', ''ŋt'', etc., are rare or unattested; an example is ''pamki'' 'legs'. The same is true when plosives appear before nasals at the ends of words or syllables. In this case, the nasal is syllabic, for example ''watn'' {{IPA|[ˈwatn̩]}} (a hardwood tree species).<ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|39}}

Plosives are generally voiced after nasals, with /p/ becoming voiced also before ''u''. At word onsets and before stressed vowels, they are aspirated and voiceless. For example: ''ɲct'' {{IPA|[ˈɪɲɟɪt]}} 'urine', ''pamki'' {{IPA|[ˈpʰamgi]}} 'legs', ''tkay'' {{IPA|[tʰəˈkʰaɪ̯]}} 'nose', ''kput'' {{IPA|[kʰɞˈbut]}} 'rain'. /p/ and /w/ weaken to a voiceless fricative: ''ipwa'' {{IPA|[iˈβa]}}. When /k/ appears before two vowels, if the second vowel is unstressed, then the /k/ is realized as a voiced fricative: ''amanakn'' {{IPA|[ʌmʌˈnaɣɨn]}} 'mine'. Intervocally /c/ has age-based [[allophony]], with older speakers preferring the stop realization and younger ones the dental sibilant [s], as in ''acak'' {{IPA|[ˈasʌk]}} 'to send'. After another consonant, /c/ is always realized as a palatal stop.<ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|39}}

/ʎ/ is in free variation between {{IPA|[ʎ]}} and {{IPA|[lʲ]}}. ''r'' varies in pronunciation between {{IPA|[l]}} and {{IPA|[ɾ]}}.<ref name=Foley/>{{Rp|40}}

=== Vowels ===

